00:00:35.711 --> 00:00:39.935
Every bit of data or information can be stored digitally. Whether it's numbers...

00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:45.255
pictures, music, or video, all of it can be represented digitally. That means the

00:00:45.255 --> 00:00:49.335
information can be represented by electrical signals that are on or off, or

00:00:49.335 --> 00:00:49.919
as one's...

00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:51.919
and zeros, so to speak.

00:00:51.919 --> 00:00:56.679
But representing information in ones and zeros can take up a lot of space.

00:00:56.679 --> 00:00:59.903
For example, if you wanted to store a three-minute song digitally...

00:00:59.903 --> 00:01:05.983
it would take up over 30 megabytes of space. A one-hour HD video would take 800 gigabytes.

00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:09.543
In the real world, digital information is compressed.

00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:15.247
to take up less space. A 30 megabyte song can be compressed down to 3 megabytes.

00:01:15.247 --> 00:01:19.727
An hour long video can be compressed from 800 gigabytes down to just 1 gigabyte.

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:25.751
of light. Sometimes compression is lossy, which means that to save some space, some

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:29.831
of the information is thrown away. For example, an image can

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:34.535
be compressed to a lower resolution to lose some detail. Lossy compression is

00:01:34.535 --> 00:01:38.275
useful because in many cases the human eye or ear can't even notice the

00:01:38.275 --> 00:01:39.839
details that are being lost.

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:45.719
When you compress data without losing any details, that's called lossless compression.

00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:49.559
This means the compressed data can be decompressed back into the exact original.

00:01:49.823 --> 00:01:55.823
One way of doing lossless compression is to find patterns in the data you're trying to compress.

00:01:55.823 --> 00:01:59.807
As an extreme example, imagine a book about dogs with high levels of fat.

00:01:59.807 --> 00:02:02.727
hundreds of pages using only one word.

00:02:02.727 --> 00:02:06.367
Each page just says dog, dog, dog, dog, dog,

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:08.527
over and over a million times.

00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:09.791
Instead of writing it all out,

00:02:09.791 --> 00:02:13.251
you could just store the pattern, dog times one million.

00:02:13.251 --> 00:02:17.651
Now that's a silly example but let's look at a more real example.

00:02:17.651 --> 00:02:19.775
Instead of a book, imagine the lyrics

00:02:19.775 --> 00:02:26.255
of a song. If a single word or phrase is repeated a lot, you can store that once and then reuse

00:02:26.255 --> 00:02:29.735
it without repeating the data.

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:31.999
with a simple text compression widget.

00:02:31.999 --> 00:02:34.919
This widget lets you experiment with compressing text.

00:02:34.919 --> 00:02:36.799
You want to represent the original text

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:39.359
with as few bytes as possible.

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:39.743
The widget is a little bit different.

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:44.743
it lets you try compressing different ways and see it as it happens. Let's see how it

00:02:44.743 --> 00:02:49.543
works. The widget shows you some text to compress. In this example, lyrics to one of

00:02:49.543 --> 00:02:49.727
Alice in the World.

00:02:49.727 --> 00:02:53.727
In the dictionary area, you can type patterns you see in the text.

00:02:53.727 --> 00:02:56.727
For now, let's just look for words that repeat.

00:02:56.727 --> 00:02:59.711
As you type in the dictionary area, a single word is a word.

00:02:59.711 --> 00:03:04.391
symbol would be substituted in the main text area. In the example here you can

00:03:04.391 --> 00:03:08.831
see each occurrence of the words "I was" has been replaced with a symbol of a sun

00:03:08.831 --> 00:03:09.695
and "I"

00:03:09.695 --> 00:03:11.695
time has been replaced with an umbrella.

00:03:19.679 --> 00:03:22.599
that they could reconstruct the original text.

00:03:22.599 --> 00:03:25.279
So the question is, is the total number

00:03:25.279 --> 00:03:27.359
of bytes in the compressed text plus the bytes

00:03:27.359 --> 00:03:29.663
in the dictionary less than the number of bytes--

00:03:29.663 --> 00:03:34.463
in the original text? The answer is yes for our current example, and the widget shows

00:03:34.463 --> 00:03:39.023
you how it's calculated. We assume that every character that needs to be sent takes one

00:03:39.023 --> 00:03:39.343
bite.

00:03:39.647 --> 00:03:47.647
So the display shows the compressed version of the lyrics with the symbols substituted in it has 216 bytes or characters.

00:03:47.647 --> 00:03:49.631
It also shows the number of bytes of the lyrics.

00:03:49.631 --> 00:03:55.751
in the dictionary as 10. This is to store the words "I was" and "I'm" also the symbols

00:03:55.751 --> 00:03:59.591
that represent those patterns. This gives us a total of 200.

00:03:59.615 --> 00:04:07.335
We can see that the original text had 240 characters or bytes, so we reduced the bytes

00:04:07.335 --> 00:04:09.575
needed to represent the poem by 14 bytes.

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:16.039
or 5.8% not a bad start. Looking for words that repeat is a good start but

00:04:16.039 --> 00:04:19.583
you can look for other patterns. Sometimes a repeating pattern

00:04:19.583 --> 00:04:23.103
could be a subpart of a word or many words.

00:04:23.103 --> 00:04:25.303
Here's where it really gets cool.

00:04:25.303 --> 00:04:27.943
You can find patterns that include dictionary symbols

00:04:27.943 --> 00:04:29.567
